+### Why the candidate cache tokens are sporadic — measured 2026-08-23
+
+The item above records *that* gemma and kimi return cache tokens unpredictably
+and asks what to do about the price. This section is the mechanism. Re-derive any
+number here with [scripts/probe_cache.py](bakeoff/scripts/probe_cache.py) rather
+than trusting it — it is a property of AWS capacity on the day you ask.
+
+**Prompt caching on the candidate arms is a lottery over a worker pool, there is
+no client-side mechanism, and — the finding that subsumes the rest — AWS SELLS NO
+PROMPT CACHING FOR THESE MODELS AT ALL, SO A CACHE HIT SAVES NOTHING.**
+
+- [x] **The pricing hole above is not a gap in the price book. It is the absence
+  of a product.** Settled 2026-08-23 against AWS's own documentation, and it
+  answers the "decide it once, for both arms" question outright:
+
+  - The Bedrock prompt-caching **supported-models table** lists Anthropic Claude
+    models, OpenAI GPT-5.6 (Sol/Terra/Luna) and — in prose — Amazon Nova.
+    **Moonshot, NVIDIA and Google appear nowhere on it.** That is the same fact
+    Converse states at runtime: `cachePoint` on `moonshotai.kimi-k2.5` or
+    `nvidia.nemotron-super-3-120b` returns *"You invoked an unsupported model or
+    your request did not allow prompt caching"*, 10 attempts out of 10.
+  - The **pricing page** shows Moonshot, NVIDIA and Google with **input and
+    output columns only — no cache-read or cache-write line exists**. Anthropic's
+    rows carry five columns including both.
+  - OpenAI usage semantics make `prompt_tokens` **inclusive** of `cached_tokens`
+    (already pinned in `tests/test_usage_accounting.py`).
+
+  Together: every input token on a candidate arm bills at the standard input
+  rate whether or not the serving layer reused its KV blocks. **A cache hit on
+  kimi, gemma or nemotron is worth exactly $0.** The `cached_tokens` field is the
+  serving layer reporting internal reuse, not a Bedrock prompt-caching hit, and
+  it has no billing consequence.
+
+  So `costs.py` raising `cache support unconfirmed` was **correct behaviour, not a
+  bug to route around** — but the reason it gave changed from "we do not know the
+  rate" to "there is no rate because there is no product," and the second reason
+  does not license the same behaviour. **Decided and encoded 2026-08-23,
+  corrected 2026-09-08:** the three candidates carry a `cache_read_multiplier` of
+  **1.0**, and `PRICING_BASIS` moved with them to
+  `sonnet-list-2026-08-11+bedrock-2026-08-05+candidate-nocache-2026-08-23`.
+  Raising was right while the rate was UNKNOWN and wrong once it was known to be
+  ABSENT — it priced good runs to `None` over tokens this book can price.
+
+  **That argument covers reads only, and 2026-09-08 put the writes back.**
+  `cache_write_multiplier` and `cache_write_1h_multiplier` are `None` again, so a
+  candidate cache *write* still raises into `pricing_error`. No candidate route
+  can produce one today — 0 of 179 stored records and 0 of 951 wire entries carry
+  a candidate cache write — so a write appearing is not a token to price, it is
+  the signal that a billed product shipped. 1.0 there would price that silently,
+  and undercharge it besides, since the only write rate anyone publishes for a
+  comparable product is Anthropic's 1.25×. (An earlier draft of this paragraph
+  said *overcharge*; that was backwards.) The read is a measurement; the write is
+  an alarm, and "silence is the enemy" decides which one a null belongs on.
+
+  **1.0 is a price, not a placeholder.** It is derived, not chosen to make a
+  number come out: from the absence of the product, and from `usage.input` being
+  exclusive of cache tokens on every route (pinned in
+  `tests/test_usage_accounting.py`). Together those make
+  `input + cache_read` at 1.0× reconstruct exactly
+  `prompt_tokens × input_per_1m`, which is what AWS bills when no cache product
+  exists.
+
+  **The risk taken, and the tripwire left on it.** If AWS later ships prompt
+  caching for one of these models, a 1.0 read multiplier silently overcharges
+  instead of loudly refusing — the failure mode inverts from loud to quiet. The
+  `None` on the write multipliers is what keeps a tripwire in place: a cache
+  product has to warm by writing, so the first billed write raises
+  `pricing_error` and names itself before any read is mispriced.
+  `PRICING_BASIS` contains the rest: it moved, so records
+  priced under the old book and the new one are distinguishable, and the token
+  counts are stored, so a re-price is a re-derivation rather than a guess.
+  Re-check with `scripts/probe_cache.py --mechanisms` before assuming this still
+  holds.
+
+  The None-means-raise mechanism is **not** removed. It is the right behaviour
+  for a model whose rate is genuinely unknown — the case it was built for — and
+  it is now pinned against a monkeypatched unknown-rate model rather than against
+  a real arm, so a future model with no published cache rate still refuses to be
+  guessed at.
+
+- [ ] **Publish candidate cost as the UNCACHED figure.** No longer a choice
+  between defensible options — with no cache product, the uncached figure is
+  simply the *correct* one. Priced honestly on the pilot workload:
+
+  | arm | prompt tokens | correct cost | what earlier notes said |
+  |---|---|---|---|
+  | kimi-k2-5 | 5,617,546 | **$3.43** | $3.05 / $2.25 |
