+
+- [ ] **Consider moving the whole matrix to `us-east-2` — for kimi's sake only.**
+  **Interleaved** replicates, region alternating call by call so region is not
+  confounded with time of day: kimi three replicates of 40 calls per region,
+  gemma two (40 and 37), nemotron one of 40. **p-values re-derived 2026-09-08**,
+  after `probe_cache.py` was found to hardcode its test direction as
+  `regions[1] > regions[0]`: it now reports a two-sided Fisher p as the headline
+  plus a one-sided p labelled with its direction. Gemma's published 0.80 was the
+  tail *opposite* its observed sign; the verdict "no effect" is unchanged.
+
+  | arm | us-east-1 | us-east-2 | verdict |
+  |---|---|---|---|
+  | kimi-k2-5 | 23/120 = 19% | **47/120 = 39%** | **confirmed.** two-sided p = 0.0010 (one-sided 0.00051 in the observed direction); all 3 replicates agree in sign (+18, +15, +27 pp) |
+  | gemma-4-31b | 15/77 = 19% | 12/77 = 16% | **no effect.** two-sided p = 0.67 (one-sided 0.34 in the observed direction); its 2 replicates disagree in sign (+5, −14 pp) |
+  | nemotron-3-super-120b | 0/40 | 0/40 | negative control — caches nowhere, and shows no region difference |
+  | claude-sonnet-5-runtime | ~97% | write→read→read, verified | contractual; unaffected |
+
+  **The dollar value of this move is $0.** With no cache product for these
+  models every input token bills at the standard rate, cached or not (see the
+  item above), so doubling the hit rate changes the bill by nothing. What it buys
+  is **latency**, and in real runs that is far smaller than the isolated probe
+  suggested: kimi's hit-vs-miss median is **1.3×** across the pilot's own wire
+  logs (4,230 ms vs 5,514 ms), not the 4.4× measured at `max_tokens=16` where
+  prefill dominated and generation did not. Gemma's is 1.0×.
+
+  So the case for moving is a modest wall-clock effect on §5.5's curve and on
+  `wall_clock_timeout_s` headroom — **not** cost. Weigh that against the §5.4
+  risk before touching it.
+
+  **Move every arm or none** — candidates in us-east-2 against Sonnet in
+  us-east-1 is precisely the §5.4 failure mode, a config choice scored as a model
+  difference. The runtime Sonnet arm works there; the *mantle* `claude-sonnet-5`
+  arm returns 404 in us-east-2 and would have to stay behind or be dropped.
+  `us-west-2` is **not** a candidate on reliability: 2 `internal_server_error`s in
+  25 calls and hit latency to 22,468 ms against us-east-2's 353–496 ms.
+
+  Interleaving removes time-of-day. It does **not** make the absolute rate
+  durable — pool occupancy is other tenants' traffic. **Re-measure before and
+  after**, or a capacity rebalance on AWS's side reads as the models getting
+  worse.
+
+  > **The first version of this item was wrong, and the way it was wrong is worth
+  > keeping.** A block design — all of region A, then all of region B, 25 calls
+  > each — reported gemma at 4% → 24% ("6×") and kimi at 21% → 44%, and put
+  > −23% on the line above. Interleaved and replicated, the gemma effect
+  > disappears: gemma's rate is just noisy, measuring 4%, then 12%, then 27% in
+  > **us-east-1 alone**. Two faults, both avoidable: the region blocks ran at
+  > different times, so region was confounded with whatever else AWS was serving
