+- [ ] **Trim the tool block.** ~16,598 tokens of every request are tool schemas —
+  99% of the first call's prompt — re-sent uncached every turn on all three
+  candidate arms. `Workflow` alone is ~5,466; `ScheduleWakeup`, `CronCreate`,
+  `EnterWorktree`, `TaskCreate`/`TaskUpdate` are ~4,700 more, none of them
+  reachable by a bug-fixing agent in a network-isolated container. Removing
+  ~10.6k/turn: kimi $3.05 → $2.41, nemotron $1.02 → $0.85, gemma $0.50 → $0.40.
+  Same §5.4 trap as the region — it changes what is being measured, so it must
+  apply to every arm identically and land *before* a collection, never between
+  arms.
+
+**The mechanism, so nobody re-derives it.** Every explicit mechanism is dead on
+the candidate arms, and each negative needed its own control:
+
+| mechanism | result |
+|---|---|
+| `cache_control` (Anthropic) | accepted and **ignored**. A 200 proves nothing — a deliberately bogus top-level key also returns 200. That swallow control is why this is not recorded as "accepted". |
+| Converse `cachePoint` | **refused 10/10**, `AccessDeniedException`: *"You invoked an unsupported model or your request did not allow prompt caching."* Same `modelId` succeeds without the block. Converse validates, so this negative stands without a control. |
+| `prompt_cache_key` (OpenAI) | no effect on hit rate |
+| connection affinity | none. Pinned keep-alive 4/25 vs fresh connection per call 4/25 — the balancer routes **per request**. |
+| dedicated capacity | unavailable. `inferenceTypesSupported` is `['ON_DEMAND']` for kimi and nemotron. |
+
+Sonnet honours `cache_control` on mantle `/anthropic` *and* `cachePoint` on
+Converse under the same probe, which is what licenses reading the candidate
+results as findings rather than as a broken harness.
+
+**What the sporadic hit actually is.** A real per-worker prefix cache behind a
+per-request balancer. Forensics on 100 stored kimi calls: every hit's `cached`
+equals an **earlier call's `prompt_tokens` in the same run**, rounded to a
+32-token block (13 of 14; the 14th is a partial match inside the shared tool
+block). Reuse lag is 2–24 calls back and is **never 1**, although call N−1's
+prompt is by construction an exact prefix of call N's — you hit when you land on
+a worker that served *some* earlier call, and which one it served sets how much
+comes back. Confirmed on byte-identical bodies: hit rate climbs with call index
+(0/10 → 5/10 over 50 calls), hits stay non-contiguous, every hit returns ~0.999,
+and a hit is 4.4× faster (477 ms vs 2100 ms median, distributions not
+overlapping).
+
+**A correction this produced.** Hit rate rises with *call index*, not prompt
+size. The two are confounded in any real run because the prompt grows every turn;
+holding size constant still produces the ramp. Any earlier note reading "large
+prompts cache better" has the variable wrong.
+
+**And one about `null`.** On kimi's `/v1` route `prompt_tokens_details` is present
+**iff** `cached_tokens > 0` — 0 of 100 stored calls carry the block with a zero in
